plain what cephalopods are, and the pair of dashes indicates that this element could be removed without affecting the grammatical coherence of the sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it fails to use appropriate punctuation to separate the supplementary element that explains what cephalopods are from the rest of the sentence. Choice B is incorrect because it fails to use appropriate punctuation to separate the supplementary element that explains what cephalopods are from the rest of the sentence. Choice C is incorrect because it fails to use appropriate punctuation to separate the supplementary element that explains what cephalopods are from the rest of the sentence.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1691007959340,"updateDate":1691007959340}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959340, 1691007959340),
    ($SATQ$2b6e1c06$SATQ$, $SATQ$1fc029ba-12f8-4fdc-8bbd-5de72b176e89$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A government body officially known as the Althing, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2b2aa67b-4aa1-4c81-b3a3-4557ecef6e8d","content":"<p>the world&rsquo;s oldest parliaments include one which first met in 930 CE, Iceland&rsquo;s.</p>"},{"id":"17bb4829-36fe-4cb7-888a-5fdd3188c9ee","content":"<p>Iceland&rsquo;s parliament is one of the oldest in the world, first meeting in 930 CE.</p>"},{"id":"3fca11f9-d0d7-4165-a46a-8d93b03b2404","content":"<p>the first meeting of one of the oldest parliaments in the world, Iceland&rsquo;s, was in 930 CE.</p>"},{"id":"2a556503-ef0e-4a4c-acf0-b76ffb4328c6","content":"<p>930 CE was the year when Iceland&rsquo;s parliament, one of the oldest parliaments in the world, first met.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["17bb4829-36fe-4cb7-888a-5fdd3188c9ee"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase "Iceland&rsquo;s parliament" the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase "a government body officially known as the Althing." In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that Iceland&rsquo;s parliament&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;is the government body known as the Althing.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase "the world&rsquo;s oldest parliaments" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the world&rsquo;s oldest parliaments are a government body known as the Althing. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase "the first meeting" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the first meeting of Iceland&rsquo;s parliament was a government body known as the Althing. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase "930 CE" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the year 930 CE is a government body known as the Althing.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2b6e1c06","external_id":"1fc029ba-12f8-4fdc-8bbd-5de72b176e89","vaultid":null,"uId":"b6d9e06a-7e34-4e92-8216-797959eda282","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A government body officially known as the Althing, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2b2aa67b-4aa1-4c81-b3a3-4557ecef6e8d","content":"<p>the world&rsquo;s oldest parliaments include one which first met in 930 CE, Iceland&rsquo;s.</p>"},{"id":"17bb4829-36fe-4cb7-888a-5fdd3188c9ee","content":"<p>Iceland&rsquo;s parliament is one of the oldest in the world, first meeting in 930 CE.</p>"},{"id":"3fca11f9-d0d7-4165-a46a-8d93b03b2404","content":"<p>the first meeting of one of the oldest parliaments in the world, Iceland&rsquo;s, was in 930 CE.</p>"},{"id":"2a556503-ef0e-4a4c-acf0-b76ffb4328c6","content":"<p>930 CE was the year when Iceland&rsquo;s parliament, one of the oldest parliaments in the world, first met.</p>"}],"keys":["17bb4829-36fe-4cb7-888a-5fdd3188c9ee"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase \"Iceland&rsquo;s parliament\" the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase \"a government body officially known as the Althing.\" In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that Iceland&rsquo;s parliament&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;is the government body known as the Althing.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase \"the world&rsquo;s oldest parliaments\" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the world&rsquo;s oldest parliaments are a government body known as the Althing. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase \"the first meeting\" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the first meeting of Iceland&rsquo;s parliament was a government body known as the Althing. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase \"930 CE\" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the year 930 CE is a government body known as the Althing.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669204,"updateDate":1743430669204}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669204, 1743430669204),
    ($SATQ$2bb7416a$SATQ$, $SATQ$2e16f638-d480-4613-b60a-1efd039dcab1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$04a216f3-ac25-4c2b-b8c3-f1567a37ee3b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In paleontology, the term &ldquo;Elvis taxon&rdquo; gets applied to a newly identified living species that was once presumed to be extinct. Like an Elvis impersonator who might bear a striking resemblance to the late musical icon Elvis Presley himself, an Elvis taxon is not the real thing, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> is a misidentified look-alike.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"bf0f997e-6239-481e-a053-e58489b2b97f","content":"<p>however but it</p>"},{"id":"ff728595-fa46-4ddd-ae52-80bdc32767f6","content":"<p>however it</p>"},{"id":"e52f2c4f-9181-4cb9-95c0-ca40ea25d389","content":"<p>however, it</p>"},{"id":"984128f1-171f-456f-953f-ef0e7dd35be9","content":"<p>however. It</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["984128f1-171f-456f-953f-ef0e7dd35be9"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The clause &ldquo;Like an Elvis impersonator&hellip;real thing&rdquo; and the clause &ldquo;it is&hellip;look-alike&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so making them into two separate sentences is grammatically correct.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice creates a run-on sentence error. The clause &ldquo;Like an Elvis impersonator&hellip;real thing&rdquo; and the clause &ldquo;it is&hellip;look-alike&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so they need to be separated with at least a comma + a coordinating conjunction. This choice provides the coordinating conjunction &ldquo;but,&rdquo; but it&rsquo;s missing a comma. Choice B is incorrect. This choice creates a run-on sentence error. The clause &ldquo;Like an Elvis impersonator&hellip;real thing&rdquo; and the clause &ldquo;it is&hellip;look-alike&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so they need to be separated with a semicolon, a colon, a dash, a period, or a comma + a coordinating conjunction. Choice C is incorrect. This choice creates a run-on sentence error. The clause &ldquo;Like an Elvis impersonator&hellip;real thing&rdquo; and the clause &ldquo;it is&hellip;look-alike&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so they need to be separated with at least a comma + a coordinating conjunction. This choice provides a comma, but it&rsquo;s missing a coordinating conjunction. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-BOU-H001$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$7fb5f3d7-436f-419d-9f2a-8992050a9971$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2bb7416a","external_id":"2e16f638-d480-4613-b60a-1efd039dcab1","vaultid":"04a216f3-ac25-4c2b-b8c3-f1567a37ee3b","uId":"67fc8b13-a903-48d8-921c-9ec92f7bd136","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In paleontology, the term &ldquo;Elvis taxon&rdquo; gets applied to a newly identified living species that was once presumed to be extinct. Like an Elvis impersonator who might bear a striking resemblance to the late musical icon Elvis Presley himself, an Elvis taxon is not the real thing, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> is a misidentified look-alike.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"bf0f997e-6239-481e-a053-e58489b2b97f","content":"<p>however but it</p>"},{"id":"ff728595-fa46-4ddd-ae52-80bdc32767f6","content":"<p>however it</p>"},{"id":"e52f2c4f-9181-4cb9-95c0-ca40ea25d389","content":"<p>however, it</p>"},{"id":"984128f1-171f-456f-953f-ef0e7dd35be9","content":"<p>however. It</p>"}],"keys":["984128f1-171f-456f-953f-ef0e7dd35be9"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The clause &ldquo;Like an Elvis impersonator&hellip;real thing&rdquo; and the clause &ldquo;it is&hellip;look-alike&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so making them into two separate sentences is grammatically correct.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice creates a run-on sentence error. The clause &ldquo;Like an Elvis impersonator&hellip;real thing&rdquo; and the clause &ldquo;it is&hellip;look-alike&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so they need to be separated with at least a comma + a coordinating conjunction. This choice provides the coordinating conjunction &ldquo;but,&rdquo; but it&rsquo;s missing a comma. Choice B is incorrect. This choice creates a run-on sentence error. The clause &ldquo;Like an Elvis impersonator&hellip;real thing&rdquo; and the clause &ldquo;it is&hellip;look-alike&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so they need to be separated with a semicolon, a colon, a dash, a period, or a comma + a coordinating conjunction. Choice C is incorrect. This choice creates a run-on sentence error. The clause &ldquo;Like an Elvis impersonator&hellip;real thing&rdquo; and the clause &ldquo;it is&hellip;look-alike&rdquo; are both independent clauses, so they need to be separated with at least a comma + a coordinating conjunction. This choice provides a comma, but it&rsquo;s missing a coordinating conjunction. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-BOU-H001","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"7fb5f3d7-436f-419d-9f2a-8992050a9971","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959358,"updateDate":1691007959358}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959358, 1691007959358),
    ($SATQ$2bca654a$SATQ$, $SATQ$6c44da23-c4c8-4327-867a-b33a5751abab$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Forming extensive networks via mycorrhizal association&mdash;that is, a symbiotic relationship between plants and fungi&mdash;<span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c171f3fa-58cd-4746-bd7e-26b0dced0238","content":"<p>it is the entanglement of pine trees&rsquo; roots and the fungus <em>Tricholoma matsutake</em>&rsquo;s fungal hyphae that makes nutrient transport possible.</p>"},{"id":"d207fd6a-0b1b-42af-ac70-8bbdf26299db","content":"<p>the transport of nutrients is possible through the entanglement of pine trees&rsquo; roots and the fungus <em>Tricholoma matsutake</em>&rsquo;s fungal hyphae.</p>"},{"id":"2d6c7881-00cf-4a74-94f7-a9cf8c4cc587","content":"<p>nutrients can be transported through the entanglement of pine trees&rsquo; roots and the fungus <em>Tricholoma matsutake</em>&rsquo;s fungal hyphae.</p>"},{"id":"971b21ce-6e5f-4f75-af16-fea70b60a4bf","content":"<p>pine trees and the fungus <em>Tricholoma matsutake</em> can transport nutrients through their entangled tree roots and fungal hyphae.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["971b21ce-6e5f-4f75-af16-fea70b60a4bf"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase "pine trees and the fungus <em>Tricholoma matsutake</em>" the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase "forming&hellip;association." In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that the pine trees and fungus&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;are forming the networks.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the it-cleft "it is" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically and confusingly suggests that "it" is forming extensive networks. Furthermore, it&rsquo;s not conventional to follow a long introductory modifying element with an it-cleft construction because it results in an unnecessarily wordy and confusing sentence, such as this. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of "the transport of nutrients" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the transport of nutrients is forming extensive networks. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of "nutrients" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that nutrients are forming extensive networks.<strong/></p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2bca654a","external_id":"6c44da23-c4c8-4327-867a-b33a5751abab","vaultid":null,"uId":"bd71d75b-6566-4537-bc16-263030aafc5d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Forming extensive networks via mycorrhizal association&mdash;that is, a symbiotic relationship between plants and fungi&mdash;<span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c171f3fa-58cd-4746-bd7e-26b0dced0238","content":"<p>it is the entanglement of pine trees&rsquo; roots and the fungus <em>Tricholoma matsutake</em>&rsquo;s fungal hyphae that makes nutrient transport possible.</p>"},{"id":"d207fd6a-0b1b-42af-ac70-8bbdf26299db","content":"<p>the transport of nutrients is possible through the entanglement of pine trees&rsquo; roots and the fungus <em>Tricholoma matsutake</em>&rsquo;s fungal hyphae.</p>"},{"id":"2d6c7881-00cf-4a74-94f7-a9cf8c4cc587","content":"<p>nutrients can be transported through the entanglement of pine trees&rsquo; roots and the fungus <em>Tricholoma matsutake</em>&rsquo;s fungal hyphae.</p>"},{"id":"971b21ce-6e5f-4f75-af16-fea70b60a4bf","content":"<p>pine trees and the fungus <em>Tricholoma matsutake</em> can transport nutrients through their entangled tree roots and fungal hyphae.</p>"}],"keys":["971b21ce-6e5f-4f75-af16-fea70b60a4bf"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase \"pine trees and the fungus <em>Tricholoma matsutake</em>\" the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase \"forming&hellip;association.\" In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that the pine trees and fungus&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;are forming the networks.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the it-cleft \"it is\" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically and confusingly suggests that \"it\" is forming extensive networks. Furthermore, it&rsquo;s not conventional to follow a long introductory modifying element with an it-cleft construction because it results in an unnecessarily wordy and confusing sentence, such as this. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of \"the transport of nutrients\" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the transport of nutrients is forming extensive networks. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of \"nutrients\" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that nutrients are forming extensive networks.<strong/></p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061178,"updateDate":1755117061178}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061178, 1755117061178),
    ($SATQ$2c49940e$SATQ$, $SATQ$609089f1-4c20-45bd-96ea-93658a00f6d8$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>French philosopher Ren&eacute; Descartes doubted whether he could prove his own existence. Eventually, he found proof in his famous phrase &ldquo;I think, therefore I am.&rdquo; The <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> complexity: only those who exist would be able to ponder their existence.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4256532b-0c2c-4708-b400-134d7387a084","content":"<p>phrases&rsquo; simplicity masks its</p>"},{"id":"fb7098bc-96b9-42a2-b8e4-bb6826f6144b","content":"<p>phrases simplicity masks their</p>"},{"id":"3579946c-8461-469e-9319-83296e18cc03","content":"<p>phrase&rsquo;s simplicity masks their</p>"},{"id":"6dab1e6f-2f2b-4e92-9171-636507df6250","content":"<p>phrase&rsquo;s simplicity masks its</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6dab1e6f-2f2b-4e92-9171-636507df6250"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The conventions being tested are the use of possessive nouns and the use of possessive determiners. The singular possessive noun "phrase&rsquo;s" correctly indicates that there is only one simple phrase. The singular possessive determiner "its" agrees in number with the singular possessive noun "phrase&rsquo;s," reinforcing the idea that there is only one simple yet complex phrase.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive noun "phrase&rsquo;s," not the plural possessive noun "phrases&rsquo;." Choice B is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive noun "phrase&rsquo;s" and the corresponding singular possessive determiner "its," not the plural noun "phrases" and the corresponding plural possessive determiner "their." Choice C is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive determiner "its," not the plural possessive determiner "their."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2c49940e","external_id":"609089f1-4c20-45bd-96ea-93658a00f6d8","vaultid":null,"uId":"055bcae3-0663-4af1-819a-8c0245a25989","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>French philosopher Ren&eacute; Descartes doubted whether he could prove his own existence. Eventually, he found proof in his famous phrase &ldquo;I think, therefore I am.&rdquo; The <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> complexity: only those who exist would be able to ponder their existence.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4256532b-0c2c-4708-b400-134d7387a084","content":"<p>phrases&rsquo; simplicity masks its</p>"},{"id":"fb7098bc-96b9-42a2-b8e4-bb6826f6144b","content":"<p>phrases simplicity masks their</p>"},{"id":"3579946c-8461-469e-9319-83296e18cc03","content":"<p>phrase&rsquo;s simplicity masks their</p>"},{"id":"6dab1e6f-2f2b-4e92-9171-636507df6250","content":"<p>phrase&rsquo;s simplicity masks its</p>"}],"keys":["6dab1e6f-2f2b-4e92-9171-636507df6250"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The conventions being tested are the use of possessive nouns and the use of possessive determiners. The singular possessive noun \"phrase&rsquo;s\" correctly indicates that there is only one simple phrase. The singular possessive determiner \"its\" agrees in number with the singular possessive noun \"phrase&rsquo;s,\" reinforcing the idea that there is only one simple yet complex phrase.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive noun \"phrase&rsquo;s,\" not the plural possessive noun \"phrases&rsquo;.\" Choice B is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive noun \"phrase&rsquo;s\" and the corresponding singular possessive determiner \"its,\" not the plural noun \"phrases\" and the corresponding plural possessive determiner \"their.\" Choice C is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive determiner \"its,\" not the plural possessive determiner \"their.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323048,"updateDate":1730147323048}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323048, 1730147323048),
    ($SATQ$2c4cd76d$SATQ$, $SATQ$ff4421fe-618a-4297-8a59-b8563e9330ce$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$d99d86a0-8555-46c9-ac32-95c3400aaa98$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Researchers studying magnetosensation have determined why some soil-dwelling roundworms in the Southern Hemisphere move in the opposite direction of Earth&rsquo;s magnetic field when searching for <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> in the Northern Hemisphere, the magnetic field points down, into the ground, but in the Southern Hemisphere, it points up, toward the surface and away from worms&rsquo; food sources.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f17728c8-e71d-4fb1-9203-da54b637ad60","content":"<p>food:</p>"},{"id":"728aac21-91cc-4258-9cf1-c2cd79373671","content":"<p>food,</p>"},{"id":"ad164911-8187-4ff1-9fab-7680463b5bf7","content":"<p>food while</p>"},{"id":"e57ace50-06d4-48d3-98a8-bcf4b03cf89e","content":"<p>food</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f17728c8-e71d-4fb1-9203-da54b637ad60"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is colon use within a sentence. A colon used in this way introduces information that illustrates or explains information that has come before it. In this case, the colon introduces the following explanation of why some roundworms in the Southern Hemisphere move in the opposite direction of Earth&rsquo;s magnetic field. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because&nbsp;it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two long independent clauses (&ldquo;Researchers&hellip;food&rdquo; and &ldquo;in&hellip;sources&rdquo;) such as these. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two clauses (&ldquo;Researchers&hellip;food&rdquo; and &ldquo;in&hellip;sources&rdquo;) are fused without punctuation. Furthermore, the conjunction &ldquo;while&rdquo; fails to indicate that what follows is an explanation of why some roundworms in the Southern Hemisphere move in the opposite direction of Earth&rsquo;s magnetic field. Choice D is incorrect because&nbsp;it results in a run-on sentence. The two clauses (&ldquo;Researchers&hellip;food&rdquo; and &ldquo;in&hellip;sources&rdquo;) are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-681-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$4716bb66-8527-4a0e-81de-a80f199e0866$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2c4cd76d","external_id":"ff4421fe-618a-4297-8a59-b8563e9330ce","vaultid":"d99d86a0-8555-46c9-ac32-95c3400aaa98","uId":"f92239de-e9a8-4b45-81ad-b89141d089c3","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Researchers studying magnetosensation have determined why some soil-dwelling roundworms in the Southern Hemisphere move in the opposite direction of Earth&rsquo;s magnetic field when searching for <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> in the Northern Hemisphere, the magnetic field points down, into the ground, but in the Southern Hemisphere, it points up, toward the surface and away from worms&rsquo; food sources.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"f17728c8-e71d-4fb1-9203-da54b637ad60","content":"<p>food:</p>"},{"id":"728aac21-91cc-4258-9cf1-c2cd79373671","content":"<p>food,</p>"},{"id":"ad164911-8187-4ff1-9fab-7680463b5bf7","content":"<p>food while</p>"},{"id":"e57ace50-06d4-48d3-98a8-bcf4b03cf89e","content":"<p>food</p>"}],"keys":["f17728c8-e71d-4fb1-9203-da54b637ad60"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is colon use within a sentence. A colon used in this way introduces information that illustrates or explains information that has come before it. In this case, the colon introduces the following explanation of why some roundworms in the Southern Hemisphere move in the opposite direction of Earth&rsquo;s magnetic field. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because&nbsp;it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two long independent clauses (&ldquo;Researchers&hellip;food&rdquo; and &ldquo;in&hellip;sources&rdquo;) such as these. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two clauses (&ldquo;Researchers&hellip;food&rdquo; and &ldquo;in&hellip;sources&rdquo;) are fused without punctuation. Furthermore, the conjunction &ldquo;while&rdquo; fails to indicate that what follows is an explanation of why some roundworms in the Southern Hemisphere move in the opposite direction of Earth&rsquo;s magnetic field. Choice D is incorrect because&nbsp;it results in a run-on sentence. The two clauses (&ldquo;Researchers&hellip;food&rdquo; and &ldquo;in&hellip;sources&rdquo;) are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-681-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"4716bb66-8527-4a0e-81de-a80f199e0866","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959346,"updateDate":1691007959346}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959346, 1691007959346),
    ($SATQ$2c84f96a$SATQ$, $SATQ$f1589ef0-65e7-4de9-a032-239112bc70fc$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$1d01e485-f8eb-4160-97d6-eafe5af55ac0$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 2017, artists Isabel and Ruben Toledo redesigned the costumes and sets for The Miami City Ballet&rsquo;s production of <em>The</em> <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> to reviewers, the Toledos&rsquo; designs helped infuse the production with elements of Miami&rsquo;s Latin American culture.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"7507b53c-d2d8-4115-9d41-862cda6ff511","content":"<p><em>Nutcracker</em> according,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"669cb4b4-0ec1-4c67-b6ff-53349b80d15a","content":"<p><em>Nutcracker</em>, according&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"eb425102-549a-455a-8abc-dfa7a99e7a8e","content":"<p><em>Nutcracker</em> according&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"f223026d-e334-47d2-9386-9f408581f0bf","content":"<p><em>Nutcracker</em>. According&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f223026d-e334-47d2-9386-9f408581f0bf"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between sentences. In this choice, the period is used correctly to mark the boundary between one sentence (&ldquo;In 2017...<em>Nutcracker</em>&rdquo;) and another (&ldquo;According...culture&rdquo;). The supplementary element &ldquo;according to reviewers&rdquo; modifies the main clause of the second sentence (&ldquo;the Toledos&rsquo;...culture&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The sentences are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Furthermore, no punctuation is needed within the supplementary element &ldquo;according to reviewers.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The sentences are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-720-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$b00656cb-8c83-495a-ac2d-0b926acf69da$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2c84f96a","external_id":"f1589ef0-65e7-4de9-a032-239112bc70fc","vaultid":"1d01e485-f8eb-4160-97d6-eafe5af55ac0","uId":"6cce992d-cbd0-408e-9548-c3846092e8c3","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 2017, artists Isabel and Ruben Toledo redesigned the costumes and sets for The Miami City Ballet&rsquo;s production of <em>The</em> <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> to reviewers, the Toledos&rsquo; designs helped infuse the production with elements of Miami&rsquo;s Latin American culture.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7507b53c-d2d8-4115-9d41-862cda6ff511","content":"<p><em>Nutcracker</em> according,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"669cb4b4-0ec1-4c67-b6ff-53349b80d15a","content":"<p><em>Nutcracker</em>, according&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"eb425102-549a-455a-8abc-dfa7a99e7a8e","content":"<p><em>Nutcracker</em> according&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"f223026d-e334-47d2-9386-9f408581f0bf","content":"<p><em>Nutcracker</em>. According&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["f223026d-e334-47d2-9386-9f408581f0bf"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between sentences. In this choice, the period is used correctly to mark the boundary between one sentence (&ldquo;In 2017...<em>Nutcracker</em>&rdquo;) and another (&ldquo;According...culture&rdquo;). The supplementary element &ldquo;according to reviewers&rdquo; modifies the main clause of the second sentence (&ldquo;the Toledos&rsquo;...culture&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The sentences are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Furthermore, no punctuation is needed within the supplementary element &ldquo;according to reviewers.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The sentences are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-720-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"b00656cb-8c83-495a-ac2d-0b926acf69da","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959348,"updateDate":1691007959348}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959348, 1691007959348),
    ($SATQ$2c9c6ca9$SATQ$, $SATQ$27e6a792-7dd0-4c78-ae6f-67d2960ac1ac$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The term &ldquo;retroflex&rdquo; derives from Latin and means &ldquo;bent back,&rdquo; an apt descriptor for the branch of consonants&mdash;retroflex consonants&mdash;pronounced with the tongue curling up and back in the mouth. In many languages, including English, these consonants are <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> in some dialects of Mandarin, however, four such consonants (&ldquo;ch,&rdquo; &ldquo;sh,&rdquo; &ldquo;zh,&rdquo; and &ldquo;r&rdquo;) are relatively common.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4ab2520f-1558-4616-ba5c-522f6c6ec8f8","content":"<p>rare and</p>"},{"id":"b98ae0b9-91d3-4dbd-ae79-1d2f84ef3c4d","content":"<p>rare<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"0d41def8-8083-4b10-8f88-dc99e1ceb5ca","content":"<p>rare</p>"},{"id":"5bfcb3b0-7326-4d71-bead-193d597d65c0","content":"<p>rare<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5bfcb3b0-7326-4d71-bead-193d597d65c0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. This choice uses a semicolon in a conventional way to join the first main clause ("In many&hellip;rare") and the second main clause ("in some&hellip;common") in this sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Joining the first main clause ("In many&hellip;rare") and the second main clause ("in some...common") with the conjunction "and" conflicts with the use of "however" later in the sentence, resulting in a confusing and illogical sentence. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2c9c6ca9","external_id":"27e6a792-7dd0-4c78-ae6f-67d2960ac1ac","vaultid":null,"uId":"510cde6b-6cbd-42e8-baec-7c65b5d28458","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The term &ldquo;retroflex&rdquo; derives from Latin and means &ldquo;bent back,&rdquo; an apt descriptor for the branch of consonants&mdash;retroflex consonants&mdash;pronounced with the tongue curling up and back in the mouth. In many languages, including English, these consonants are <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> in some dialects of Mandarin, however, four such consonants (&ldquo;ch,&rdquo; &ldquo;sh,&rdquo; &ldquo;zh,&rdquo; and &ldquo;r&rdquo;) are relatively common.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4ab2520f-1558-4616-ba5c-522f6c6ec8f8","content":"<p>rare and</p>"},{"id":"b98ae0b9-91d3-4dbd-ae79-1d2f84ef3c4d","content":"<p>rare<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"0d41def8-8083-4b10-8f88-dc99e1ceb5ca","content":"<p>rare</p>"},{"id":"5bfcb3b0-7326-4d71-bead-193d597d65c0","content":"<p>rare<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span></p>"}],"keys":["5bfcb3b0-7326-4d71-bead-193d597d65c0"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. This choice uses a semicolon in a conventional way to join the first main clause (\"In many&hellip;rare\") and the second main clause (\"in some&hellip;common\") in this sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Joining the first main clause (\"In many&hellip;rare\") and the second main clause (\"in some...common\") with the conjunction \"and\" conflicts with the use of \"however\" later in the sentence, resulting in a confusing and illogical sentence. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669204,"updateDate":1743430669204}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669204, 1743430669204),
    ($SATQ$2dd1b8bf$SATQ$, $SATQ$89f889f8-33b2-4456-a14a-eca50b5f9f38$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$10932649-d480-480c-9a23-11e9604e6e5b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Compared to that of alumina glass, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> silica glass atoms are so far apart that they are unable to re-form bonds after being separated.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3e82b9c6-a81e-4778-8076-11b3f40a6a04","content":"<p>silica glass is at a significant disadvantage due to its more dispersed atomic arrangement:</p>"},{"id":"d15e3798-5fad-42a2-8613-a827345fdc36","content":"<p>silica glass has a more dispersed atomic arrangement, resulting in a significant disadvantage:</p>"},{"id":"472facd9-a0e8-47e8-a0f0-db2ab57ca4bd","content":"<p>a significant disadvantage of silica glass is that its atomic arrangement is more dispersed:</p>"},{"id":"368ba6b8-5637-4898-b996-1ed095770987","content":"<p>silica glass&rsquo;s atomic arrangement is more dispersed, resulting in a significant disadvantage:</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["368ba6b8-5637-4898-b996-1ed095770987"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes &ldquo;silica glass&rsquo;s atomic arrangement&rdquo; the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase &ldquo;compared to that of alumina glass.&rdquo; In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that silica glass&rsquo;s atomic arrangement&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;is being compared to the atomic arrangement (&ldquo;that&rdquo;) of alumina glass. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;silica glass&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that silica glass itself (rather than its atomic arrangement) is being compared to alumina glass&rsquo;s atomic arrangement. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;silica glass&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that silica glass itself (rather than its atomic arrangement) is being compared to alumina glass&rsquo;s atomic arrangement. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;a significant disadvantage&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that &ldquo;a significant disadvantage&rdquo; is being compared to alumina glass&rsquo;s atomic arrangement. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-674-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$80ce55ba-bd0f-485f-814d-da7eec20c258$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2dd1b8bf","external_id":"89f889f8-33b2-4456-a14a-eca50b5f9f38","vaultid":"10932649-d480-480c-9a23-11e9604e6e5b","uId":"85da3ecb-5bff-4c96-b3b8-56589c011ba4","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Compared to that of alumina glass, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> silica glass atoms are so far apart that they are unable to re-form bonds after being separated.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3e82b9c6-a81e-4778-8076-11b3f40a6a04","content":"<p>silica glass is at a significant disadvantage due to its more dispersed a