 alumina glass&rsquo;s atomic arrangement. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-674-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"80ce55ba-bd0f-485f-814d-da7eec20c258","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959346,"updateDate":1691007959346}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959346, 1691007959346),
    ($SATQ$2ee50d41$SATQ$, $SATQ$cf0c5d1f-d0c9-4136-8b34-dcc2ebaf79e2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$ad136a34-f4e9-411c-8e3f-49e0eb2ca97f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The classic children&rsquo;s board game Chutes and Ladders is a version of an ancient Nepalese game, Paramapada Sopanapata<em>.</em> In both games, players encounter &ldquo;good&rdquo; or &ldquo;bad&rdquo; spaces while traveling along a path; landing on one of the good spaces <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> a player to skip ahead and arrive closer to the end goal.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f03d0142-8570-4c78-9c61-16d90630bc2b","content":"<p>allows</p>"},{"id":"a829d8c1-87ff-4133-af5b-c730848eaa87","content":"<p>are allowing</p>"},{"id":"d162f00b-861b-4164-a2f4-9c103a1bbddb","content":"<p>have allowed</p>"},{"id":"56aeba4d-a75b-42ae-8bc1-4c1dc9678edf","content":"<p>allow</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f03d0142-8570-4c78-9c61-16d90630bc2b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject&ndash;verb agreement. The singular verb &ldquo;allows&rdquo; agrees in number with the singular subject &ldquo;landing.&rdquo; </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb &ldquo;are allowing&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject &ldquo;landing.&rdquo; Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb &ldquo;have allowed&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject &ldquo;landing.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect because the plural verb &ldquo;allow&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject &ldquo;landing.&rdquo; </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $S