ubject &ldquo;landing.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect because the plural verb &ldquo;allow&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject &ldquo;landing.&rdquo; </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-621-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"9d0c6b48-d23c-44a5-9007-f52da5071e2e","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959339,"updateDate":1691007959339}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959339, 1691007959339),
    ($SATQ$2fd05c15$SATQ$, $SATQ$8a9f9154-be7d-4115-914f-7de2a4e80f23$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$8d7715a8-4318-4ba1-a6a3-154861902dc8$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In crafting her fantasy fiction, Nigerian-born British author Helen Oyeyemi has drawn inspiration from the classic nineteenth-century fairy tales of the Brothers Grimm. Her 2014 novel <em>Boy, Snow, Bird</em>, for instance, is a complex retelling of the story of Snow White, while her 2019 novel <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> offers a delicious twist on the classic tale of Hansel and Gretel. </p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d7062477-5050-4d3f-919e-70cb656748d2","content":"<p><em>&shy;&shy;Gingerbread</em>&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"f177eb84-cf70-4f13-8618-bd7d419a7ac9","content":"<p><em>&shy;&shy;Gingerbread,</em></p>"},{"id":"c3584611-0f71-47e9-bb56-83baf9dc1e1a","content":"<p><em>Gingerbread</em></p>"},{"id":"6098756a-3204-40b0-80b4-ab8927613b2b","content":"<p><em>&shy;&shy;Gingerbread:</em></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c3584611-0f71-47e9-bb56-83baf9dc1e1a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a subject and a verb. When, as in this case, a subject (&ldquo;her 2019 novel <em>Gingerbread</em>&rdquo;) is immediately followed by a verb (&ldquo;offers&rdquo;), no punctuation is needed. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-619-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$bca03ab4-ae7b-4761-8619-8c1251f33024$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2fd05c15","external_id":"8a9f9154-be7d-4115-914f-7de2a4e80f23","vaultid":"8d7715a8-4318-4ba1-a6a3-154861902dc8","uId":"a8630e0d-8f28-4a03-9596-e4cee455dfee","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In crafting her fantasy fiction, Nigerian-born British author Helen Oyeyemi has drawn inspiration from the classic nineteenth-century fairy tales of the Brothers Grimm. Her 2014 novel <em>Boy, Snow, Bird</em>, for instance, is a complex retelling of the story of Snow White, while her 2019 novel <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> offers a delicious twist on the classic tale of Hansel and Gretel. </p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d7062477-5050-4d3f-919e-70cb656748d2","content":"<p><em>&shy;&shy;Gingerbread</em>&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"f177eb84-cf70-4f13-8618-bd7d419a7ac9","content":"<p><em>&shy;&shy;Gingerbread,</em></p>"},{"id":"c3584611-0f71-47e9-bb56-83baf9dc1e1a","content":"<p><em>Gingerbread</em></p>"},{"id":"6098756a-3204-40b0-80b4-ab8927613b2b","content":"<p><em>&shy;&shy;Gingerbread:</em></p>"}],"keys":["c3584611-0f71-47e9-bb56-83baf9dc1e1a"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a subject and a verb. When, as in this case, a subject (&ldquo;her 2019 novel <em>Gingerbread</em>&rdquo;) is immediately followed by a verb (&ldquo;offers&rdquo;), no punctuation is needed. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-619-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"bca03ab4-ae7b-4761-8619-8c1251f33024","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959339,"updateDate":1691007959339}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959339, 1691007959339),
    ($SATQ$31362d2d$SATQ$, $SATQ$08df22f2-5ba9-4073-bf61-ba5e2563f16b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$2e2c3a09-8610-472c-9203-28c2a9ab14dc$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>If simple sugars such as ribose and glycolaldehyde <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Earth from elsewhere and survived impact&mdash;a possibility astrophysicist Nicolle Zellner outlined in a 2020 study&mdash;the sugars could have reacted with other molecules that were already present on the planet to form the nucleotides that are the structural components of RNA and DNA.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"68047f2c-6015-4d88-b595-52b3aa4a0eba","content":"<p>reach</p>"},{"id":"1765c2bf-0276-44b9-bb20-57f590c1aaab","content":"<p>had reached</p>"},{"id":"a5874113-5eb1-4cbe-a2c0-40dd74bf27f0","content":"<p>will reach</p>"},{"id":"94b52a2e-6ab4-4d39-8268-5e6ad46a8874","content":"<p>are reaching</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1765c2bf-0276-44b9-bb20-57f590c1aaab"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verbs to express tense. In this choice, the past perfect verb &ldquo;had reached&rdquo; is used correctly to describe a past action that was completed before another action in the past. Specifically, in this hypothetical scenario about the origins of RNA and DNA on Earth, the simple sugars had to have reached Earth before they could react with other molecules on the planet. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the present tense verb &ldquo;reach&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the simple sugars reached Earth before reacting with other molecules on the planet. Choice C is incorrect because the future tense verb &ldquo;will reach&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the simple sugars reached Earth before reacting with other molecules on the planet. Choice D is incorrect because the present progressive verb &ldquo;are reaching&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the simple sugars reached Earth before reacting with other molecules on the planet. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-646-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$bc544391-c3ee-4463-9611-4112aa5630eb$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"31362d2d","external_id":"08df22f2-5ba9-4073-bf61-ba5e2563f16b","vaultid":"2e2c3a09-8610-472c-9203-28c2a9ab14dc","uId":"ba50c8ae-0fa6-4f0b-96a7-4f0e9b4d30a1","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>If simple sugars such as ribose and glycolaldehyde <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Earth from elsewhere and survived impact&mdash;a possibility astrophysicist Nicolle Zellner outlined in a 2020 study&mdash;the sugars could have reacted with other molecules that were already present on the planet to form the nucleotides that are the structural components of RNA and DNA.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"68047f2c-6015-4d88-b595-52b3aa4a0eba","content":"<p>reach</p>"},{"id":"1765c2bf-0276-44b9-bb20-57f590c1aaab","content":"<p>had reached</p>"},{"id":"a5874113-5eb1-4cbe-a2c0-40dd74bf27f0","content":"<p>will reach</p>"},{"id":"94b52a2e-6ab4-4d39-8268-5e6ad46a8874","content":"<p>are reaching</p>"}],"keys":["1765c2bf-0276-44b9-bb20-57f590c1aaab"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verbs to express tense. In this choice, the past perfect verb &ldquo;had reached&rdquo; is used correctly to describe a past action that was completed before another action in the past. Specifically, in this hypothetical scenario about the origins of RNA and DNA on Earth, the simple sugars had to have reached Earth before they could react with other molecules on the planet. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the present tense verb &ldquo;reach&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the simple sugars reached Earth before reacting with other molecules on the planet. Choice C is incorrect because the future tense verb &ldquo;will reach&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the simple sugars reached Earth before reacting with other molecules on the planet. Choice D is incorrect because the present progressive verb &ldquo;are reaching&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the simple sugars reached Earth before reacting with other molecules on the planet. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-646-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"bc544391-c3ee-4463-9611-4112aa5630eb","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959340,"updateDate":1691007959340}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959340, 1691007959340),
    ($SATQ$329255db$SATQ$, $SATQ$b22ff9c2-896d-4eac-8572-8d6192510249$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$557d306c-e1f5-4f80-810e-b7c33863c5c6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Bengali author Toru Dutt&rsquo;s <em>A Sheaf Gleaned in French Fields </em>(1876), a volume of English translations of French poems, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> scholars&rsquo; understanding of the transnational and multilingual contexts in which Dutt lived and worked.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ccadaf18-5f12-4952-a9ff-0d6527d8de72","content":"<p>has enhanced</p>"},{"id":"8e4428c3-62a3-404a-afe8-9f45383c13d8","content":"<p>are enhancing</p>"},{"id":"45267709-b4b5-4c96-81c8-3c312007b644","content":"<p>have enhanced</p>"},{"id":"1e6204d6-e3c7-4c9f-9d80-2080aa66434e","content":"<p>enhance</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ccadaf18-5f12-4952-a9ff-0d6527d8de72"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb &ldquo;has enhanced&rdquo; agrees in number with the singular subject &ldquo;<em>A Sheaf Gleaned in French Fields,</em>&rdquo; which is the title of a book of poems. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb &ldquo;are enhancing&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject &ldquo;<em>A Sheaf Gleaned in French Fields</em>.&rdquo; Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb &ldquo;have enhanced&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject &ldquo;<em>A Sheaf Gleaned in French Fields</em>.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect because the plural verb &ldquo;enhance&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject &ldquo;<em>A Sheaf Gleaned in French Fields</em>.&rdquo; </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-667-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$cf541e5c-b261-4f7e-ae26-2712b6272b8e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"329255db","external_id":"b22ff9c2-896d-4eac-8572-8d6192510249","vaultid":"557d306c-e1f5-4f80-810e-b7c33863c5c6","uId":"c737018f-6632-4290-a99b-4daa0a90b138","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Bengali author Toru Dutt&rsquo;s <em>A Sheaf Gleaned in French Fields </em>(1876), a volume of English translations of French poems, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> scholars&rsquo; understanding of the transnational and multilingual contexts in which Dutt lived and worked.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ccadaf18-5f12-4952-a9ff-0d6527d8de72","content":"<p>has enhanced</p>"},{"id":"8e4428c3-62a3-404a-afe8-9f45383c13d8","content":"<p>are enhancing</p>"},{"id":"45267709-b4b5-4c96-81c8-3c312007b644","content":"<p>have enhanced</p>"},{"id":"1e6204d6-e3c7-4c9f-9d80-2080aa66434e","content":"<p>enhance</p>"}],"keys":["ccadaf18-5f12-4952-a9ff-0d6527d8de72"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb &ldquo;has enhanced&rdquo; agrees in number with the singular subject &ldquo;<em>A Sheaf Gleaned in French Fields,</em>&rdquo; which is the title of a book of poems. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb &ldquo;are enhancing&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject &ldquo;<em>A Sheaf Gleaned in French Fields</em>.&rdquo; Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb &ldquo;have enhanced&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject &ldquo;<em>A Sheaf Gleaned in French Fields</em>.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect because the plural verb &ldquo;enhance&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject &ldquo;<em>A Sheaf Gleaned in French Fields</em>.&rdquo; </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-667-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"cf541e5c-b261-4f7e-ae26-2712b6272b8e","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959345,"updateDate":1691007959345}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959345, 1691007959345),
    ($SATQ$333b2b65$SATQ$, $SATQ$dacdeae0-91cc-4045-b9ef-dc27fb85d045$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$814fecd8-05d6-4d22-8d84-3b91a4241d24$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While one requires oxygen and one does <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> and anaerobic respiration are both forms of cellular respiration&mdash;that is, they are processes by which cells break down glucose to use as energy.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ef9915b9-8d69-45d3-9797-10202fa9e939","content":"<p>not aerobic</p>"},{"id":"89375c01-a072-4ed5-8599-1140ce2f4fe1","content":"<p>&nbsp;not. Aerobic</p>"},{"id":"54382b1a-c6e9-40e7-bcf4-85b1a9bae48c","content":"<p>not, aerobic</p>"},{"id":"15027418-c730-4d61-8216-d900016f98d5","content":"<p>&nbsp;not; aerobic</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["54382b1a-c6e9-40e7-bcf4-85b1a9bae48c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. A comma is the appropriate way to link the dependent clause &ldquo;While...not&rdquo; and the independent clause that follows. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice creates a run-on sentence error. &ldquo;While...not&rdquo; is a dependent clause, which must be separated from the independent clause that follows with some sort of punctuation. Choice B is incorrect. This choice creates a sentence fragment. &ldquo;While one requires oxygen and one does not&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t an independent clause, so it can&rsquo;t stand alone as a complete sentence. Choice D is incorrect. This choice creates a punctuation error. &ldquo;While one requires oxygen and one does not&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t an independent clause, so it can&rsquo;t be linked to the clause that follows with a semicolon. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-BOU-E003$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$df3eda65-fe30-42cb-afde-08e603e6e062$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"333b2b65","external_id":"dacdeae0-91cc-4045-b9ef-dc27fb85d045","vaultid":"814fecd8-05d6-4d22-8d84-3b91a4241d24","uId":"0f65923d-f479-44f3-9c25-83ad43e19bba","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While one requires oxygen and one does <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> and anaerobic respiration are both forms of cellular respiration&mdash;that is, they are processes by which cells break down glucose to use as energy.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ef9915b9-8d69-45d3-9797-10202fa9e939","content":"<p>not aerobic</p>"},{"id":"89375c01-a072-4ed5-8599-1140ce2f4fe1","content":"<p>&nbsp;not. Aerobic</p>"},{"id":"54382b1a-c6e9-40e7-bcf4-85b1a9bae48c","content":"<p>not, aerobic</p>"},{"id":"15027418-c730-4d61-8216-d900016f98d5","content":"<p>&nbsp;not; aerobic</p>"}],"keys":["54382b1a-c6e9-40e7-bcf4-85b1a9bae48c"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. A comma is the appropriate way to link the dependent clause &ldquo;While...not&rdquo; and the independent clause that follows. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice creates a run-on sentence error. &ldquo;While...not&rdquo; is a dependent clause, which must be separated from the independent clause that follows with some sort of punctuation. Choice B is incorrect. This choice creates a sentence fragment. &ldquo;While one requires oxygen and one does not&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t an independent clause, so it can&rsquo;t stand alone as a complete sentence. Choice D is incorrect. This choice creates a punctuation error. &ldquo;While one requires oxygen and one does not&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t an independent clause, so it can&rsquo;t be linked to the clause that follows with a semicolon. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-BOU-E003","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"df3eda65-fe30-42cb-afde-08e603e6e062","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959358,"updateDate":1691007959358}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959358, 1691007959358),
    ($SATQ$35360da9$SATQ$, $SATQ$b8794e86-cc8f-479a-8a91-f54d81a14747$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$2ce19175-a319-4883-9b68-2b59d4bd4661$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The US Geological Survey wants to map every human-made structure in the United States, and it is asking volunteers to help. Cassie Tammy Wang and Ashish D&rsquo;Souza are just two of the many volunteer map editors who <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> to the project since it began in 2012.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ef812a42-1a00-452e-a3e2-3c1f9f878148","content":"<p>contribute</p>"},{"id":"29be742e-25e4-4250-95ce-b89caf3e86dd","content":"<p>will contribute</p>"},{"id":"a20e5af4-726a-45f6-8796-ba0063537c15","content":"<p>have contributed</p>"},{"id":"d415385b-1c93-424c-9a2f-83baf61ddf38","content":"<p>will be contributing</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a20e5af4-726a-45f6-8796-ba0063537c15"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verbs to express tense in a sentence. In this choice, the present perfect tense verb &ldquo;have contributed,&rdquo; used in conjunction with the phrase &ldquo;since it began in 2012,&rdquo; correctly indicates that map editors have contributed in the past and continue to do so in the present. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the present tense verb &ldquo;contribute&rdquo; is inconsistent with the phrase &ldquo;since it began in 2012,&rdquo; which suggests that the contributions occurred in the past and continue into the present. Choice B is incorrect because the future tense verb &ldquo;will contribute&rdquo; is inconsistent with the phrase &ldquo;since it began in 2012,&rdquo; which suggests that the contributions occurred in the past and continue into the present. Choice D is incorrect because the future tense verb &ldquo;will be contributing&rdquo; is inconsistent with the phrase &ldquo;since it began in 2012,&rdquo; which suggests that the contributions occurred in the past and continue into the present. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-755-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$5f7c7ced-018f-431c-88ca-0b30433626f1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"35360da9","external_id":"b8794e86-cc8f-479a-8a91-f54d81a14747","vaultid":"2ce19175-a319-4883-9b68-2b59d4bd4661","uId":"6f7df66b-27d5-4c03-8db5-aaffdf593c31","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The US Geological Survey wants to map every human-made structure in the United States, and it is asking volunteers to help. Cassie Tammy Wang and Ashish D&rsquo;Souza are just two of the many volunteer map editors who <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> to the project since it began in 2012.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ef812a42-1a00-452e-a3e2-3c1f9f878148","content":"<p>contribute</p>"},{"id":"29be742e-25e4-4250-95ce-b89caf3e86dd","content":"<p>will contribute</p>"},{"id":"a20e5af4-726a-45f6-8796-ba0063537c15","content":"<p>have contributed</p>"},{"id":"d415385b-1c93-424c-9a2f-83baf61ddf38","content":"<p>will be contributing</p>"}],"keys":["a20e5af4-726a-45f6-8796-ba0063537c15"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verbs to express tense in a sentence. In this choice, the present perfect tense verb &ldquo;have contributed,&rdquo; used in conjunction with the phrase &ldquo;since it began in 2012,&rdquo; correctly indicates that map editors have contributed in the past and continue to do so in the present. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the present tense verb &ldquo;contribute&rdquo; is inconsistent with the phrase &ldquo;since it began in 2012,&rdquo; which suggests that the contributions occurred in the past and continue into the present. Choice B is incorrect because the future tense verb &ldquo;will contribute&rdquo; is inconsistent with the phrase &ldquo;since it began in 2012,&rdquo; which suggests that the contributions occurred in the past and continue into the present. Choice D is incorrect because the future tense verb &ldquo;will be contributing&rdquo; is inconsistent with the phrase &ldquo;since it began in 2012,&rdquo; which suggests that the contributions occurred in the past and continue into the present. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-755-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"5f7c7ced-018f-431c-88ca-0b30433626f1","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959351,"updateDate":1691007959351}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959351, 1691007959351),
    ($SATQ$3580533b$SATQ$, $SATQ$da5f48d2-3276-4469-85e5-9116ec9afd2c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$8726b441-ce9f-457e-b6a8-f9c789a89b49$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In recent years, economists around the world have created new tools that quantify the overall well-being of a country&rsquo;s citizens. Economists in India, for example, use an Ease of Living Index. This tool <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> economic potential, sustainability, and citizens&rsquo; quality of life.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ed1a4794-17c5-4722-b1f1-228f12b17e3d","content":"<p>measures</p>"},{"id":"8cc3010c-0285-4ca2-98bd-01bf08d24bb9","content":"<p>had measured</p>"},{"id":"55dd2267-e03b-40bb-85c0-a2731a117542","content":"<p>would have measured</p>"},{"id":"b3982f1a-35cf-4910-a5b4-e6e4165a0b50","content":"<p>will have been measuring</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ed1a4794-17c5-4722-b1f1-228f12b17e3d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The previous sentence tells us how economists in India "use" a certain tool, while this sentence describes general facts about that tool. To express general facts (and also to match the simple present tense of "use"), we should use the simple present tense form "measures."</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This choice uses the past perfect tense, but the previous sentence tells us that the tool is currently used to measure things, so the past tense doesn&rsquo;t make sense for this verb. Choice C is incorrect. This choice uses the future perfect conditional tense, but the previous sentence tells us that the tool is currently used to measure things, so the future tense doesn&rsquo;t make sense for this verb. Choice D is incorrect. This choice uses the future perfect continuous tense, but the previous sentence tells us that the tool is currently used to measure things, so the future tense doesn&rsquo;t make sense for this verb.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-FSS-E007$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$7ba0c133-bd44-43f8-8185-a556872fad3f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3580533b","external_id":"da5f48d2-3276-4469-85e5-9116ec9afd2c","vaultid":"8726b441-ce9f-457e-b6a8-f9c789a89b49","uId":"054b24aa-e28f-44c8-96cc-c6a47520f856","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In recent years, economists around the world have created new tools that quantify the overall well-being of a country&rsquo;s citizens. Economists in India, for example, use an Ease of Living Index. This tool <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> economic potential, sustainability, and citizens&rsquo; quality of life.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ed1a4794-17c5-4722-b1f1-228f12b17e3d","content":"<p>measures</p>"},{"id":"8cc3010c-0285-4ca2-98bd-01bf08d24bb9","content":"<p>had measured</p>"},{"id":"55dd2267-e03b-40bb-85c0-a2731a117542","content":"<p>would have measured</p>"},{"id":"b3982f1a-35cf-4910-a5b4-e6e4165a0b50","content":"<p>will have been measuring</p>"}],"keys":["ed1a4794-17c5-4722-b1f1-228f12b17e3d"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The previous sentence tells us how economists in India \"use\" a certain tool, while this sentence describes general facts about that tool. To express general facts (and also to match the simple present tense of \"use\"), we should use the simple present tense form \"measures.\"</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This choice uses the past perfect tense, but the previous sentence tells us that the tool is currently used to measure things, so the past tense doesn&rsquo;t make sense for this verb. Choice C is incorrect. This choice uses the future perfect conditional tense, but the previous sentence tells us that the tool is currently used to measure things, so the future tense doesn&rsquo;t make sense for this verb. Choice D is incorrect. This choice uses the future perfect continuous tense, but the previous sentence tells us that the tool is currently used to measure things, so the future tense doesn&rsquo;t make sense for this verb.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-FSS-E007","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"7ba0c133-bd44-43f8-8185-a556872fad3f","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959366,"updateDate":1691007959366}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959366, 1691007959366)
